# Community Care Initiative Brief 
(As of April, 2026)

## Outcome Summary
The goal is to enable Veterans to submit claims for their Community Care appointments. A Community Care appointment is an appointment scheduled at a non-VAMC appointment.  Confirmation that the Veeran attended their appointment must be supplied with their claim submissioon in order to receive reimbursement. The current goal is for this flow to exist on VA.gov with a low error rate.

## Problem
* Less than 30$ of claims are related to Community Care appointments. To file these claims online, Veterans must be able to authenticate separately into the BTSSS portal, submit confirmation they attended their appointments alongside any additional receipts for their travel expenses. 
* This initiative enables Veterans to navigate to and file for travel claims under one (VA.gov) authentication, and will provide a seamless way for them to file for travel reimbursement for their appointmens at autorized local providers.

## Desired User Outcomes
* Users are able to file travel claims for simple, mileage-only claims (SMOC) and claims with individual receipts for authorized LOCAL providers on the VA.GOV website without authenticating onto the BTSSS portal 
* More users will be able to find and file for travel expense reimbursement.

## Undesired User Outcomes
* Users encounter a high error rate while filing travel reimbursement claims.
* Users cannot find the starting point for travel claims submission.

## Desired Business Outcomes
- Increase in total volume of SMOC filings.
- Increase in total volume of Complex Claims filings.
- Increase in total volume of Community Care filings.
- Increase in total volume of claims submitted via BTSSS YoY.
 
## Undesired Business Outcomes
- Higher rate of manual review claims than on existing BTSSS portal
- Continued usage of BTSSS portal for SMOC-eligible claims (high cost)

